{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 14, :comments ["<b>Or when you buy from the hand of your fellow — you shall not wrong one another.</b> Our Sages, of blessed memory (Bava Metzi'a 47b), interpreted that a thing acquired hand to hand is subject to עוֹנָאָה (overreaching), but for land there is no עוֹנָאָה. And the reason for this is that every thing sold is not at one fixed value across all times, for according to the change of the seasons it sometimes becomes dearer or cheaper; therefore there is never a known value for any thing sold. Consequently, land — even though at the time it was there it may have been sold cheaply or dearly — none the less it cannot be avoided that a time will come when it will be sold at the same value at which one is buying it now. But movable goods — one must be concerned lest at that time they no longer exist in the world, whereas the earth endures forever. And [the wronging of land] is not [punished] by a quarter-[-shekel threshold] as with land, but only by nullification of the sale; none the less there is a prohibition in the matter, for behold it is stated concerning land, ״בְּמִסְפַּר שָׁנִים אַחַר הַיּוֹבֵל״, and concerning this He returned and said, ״וְלֹא תוֹנוּ״."], :book :leviticus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:38:27.420126227Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}